WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.640
All right, that's too bad, always. I'm thrilled&nbsp;
to hear from our Scottish callers, why don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:03.120
we go to Nick from Tennessee&nbsp;
next. Nick, you're on the air.&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:12.240
Nick from Tennessee, you're on the air.&nbsp;
Hey, David, can you hear me? Yes, I can.&nbsp;

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:20.000
Hey, thanks for taking my call. My question is&nbsp;
actually about the stock market. It's something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:27.520
that I've taken an interest in, mainly after&nbsp;
reading Matt Taibbi s journalistic work on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:34.800
2008 financial crisis and learning more through&nbsp;
the documentary Capital in the Twenty First&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:43.680
Century. And my question for you is, is&nbsp;
it am I overestimating the impact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:49.120
the stock market and financial market has on our&nbsp;
system? Or is it something that doesn't get talked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:54.480
about due to these sort of inaccessibility&nbsp;
of it and how hard it is to enact change?&nbsp;

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:03.200
Well, you might have to say a bit. No, I think I&nbsp;
think many people accurately understand the impact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:09.520
of the financial system, the stock market being a&nbsp;
piece of that on our political system. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.760
maybe you could be more specific in terms of the&nbsp;
ways you think are not appreciated enough. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.760
I think anybody who's paying attention to the&nbsp;
political space in a serious way understands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.920
that the financial system is inextricably&nbsp;
linked to the political system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.200
both in terms of how the financial system&nbsp;
funds the political system, but also how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:33.440
it has created sort of this revolving&nbsp;
door of regulatory capture, where you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:39.440
the regulators and the industry leaders and&nbsp;
the elected officials all together and often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:44.560
going from one role to the next. Is there a&nbsp;
specific way you think it's underappreciated?&nbsp;

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:54.320
I know it's tough because I'm just learning about&nbsp;
it, so I'm still sort of finding the excuse me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.320 --> 00:02:05.920
the vocabulary to. To express my concern with it,&nbsp;
but, yeah, and learning just the history of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:14.480
the stock market essentially took over the economy&nbsp;
as soon as it was introduced or created. And, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:19.920
know, around the time just after industrialization&nbsp;
in America and the fact that it has sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:27.200
remained this very powerful presence but doesn't&nbsp;
seem to ever enter the the political arena of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:33.760
discussion, at least on the level of public&nbsp;
discourse, which we hear from most media sources.&nbsp;

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:38.160
Yeah, and I think I think to some degree,&nbsp;
you're right. I mean, I do think that there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:43.200
a superficial analysis of if the stock&nbsp;
market's up, it signals a good economy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.640
And if it's down, it signals a bad economy. And&nbsp;
of course, the stock market isn't the economy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:54.400
stocks are disproportionately held by a relatively&nbsp;
small percentage of people, certainly the vast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.400 --> 00:03:00.080
vast majority of the shares. But I think I would&nbsp;
agree with you that there's not a deeper analysis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:04.640
of the connection between the stock market and the&nbsp;
political system that that maybe would be useful.&nbsp;

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:07.040
But I do think most people&nbsp;
are at least generally aware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:11.920
of the role of the stock market and how publicly&nbsp;
traded companies, generally speaking, operate.&nbsp;

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:20.320
That's fair, and like I said, I'm still learning&nbsp;
and new to this world, but it's just something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:26.080
that has really intrigued me lately. And&nbsp;
just one final question for you. And I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:32.320
learned through watching your content that you&nbsp;
participate in the stock market. What is your,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:40.240
I guess, stance on it as a participant from our&nbsp;
on the ground level of being just a consumer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:46.080
who is also involved in stocks and investing?
Yes. So this isn't financial advice advice. But as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:52.640
I've said before, you know, my my full time thing&nbsp;
is not picking stocks and making investments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:58.880
And even the people who do that full time often&nbsp;
underperform the market averages. So I'm not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:04.320
arrogant enough to think I'm particularly&nbsp;
good at picking stocks. So my approach is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:11.840
dollar cost averaging regularly buying more&nbsp;
and more shares of low cost index funds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:17.920
which you can get for sometimes five basis points,&nbsp;
meaning point zero five percent fees. Nope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:23.600
not paying any managers one one and a half&nbsp;
percent fees for for managing a hedge fund.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:30.160
Just go with the lowest price index funds.&nbsp;
And then within my portfolio, maybe three to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:35.840
five percent of my money is in individual stocks&nbsp;
that I have some knowledge of. So, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:40.560
people know I bought Tesla's stock six years&nbsp;
ago and I have a few other individual stocks.&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:46.800
But my approach as a participant is I'm busy&nbsp;
hosting my show, running a business. I devote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:51.200
very little time to picking stocks&nbsp;
and I'm just buying the broader index&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:59.440
funds and paying as little as possible in fees.
Cool. Thanks a lot for taking my call. I really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:03.840
enjoy your show and I look forward to tomorrow.
All right. My pleasure. Nick from Tennessee.

